# GC186 Fresh blood in stool, lower GI bleeding

## Clinical approach to PR bleeding

- Types of lower GI bleeding:
  - Intermittent/ episodic PR bleeding - outlet type bleeding (light bright red, not mixed w/ stool):
    - Majority benign (\>90%) - haemarrhoids, anal fissures, polyps, proctitis, rectal ulcers
    - Others malignant - Anal cancer
  - Acute LGIB
  - Gastrointestinal bleeding of obscure origin
- Salient points of Hx:
  - HPI - onset, severity (does it come w/ clots), previous bleeding episodes +/- Ix (e.g. prior colonoscopy)
  - Associated Sx:
    - GI Sx - abd pain and altered bowel habits
    - Anorectal Sx - prolapse, perianal pain
    - Systemic Sx
  - PMH - significant co-morbid conditions - heart and liver disease
  - Drug Hx - NSAID, anti-thrombotic agents
  - SHx
  - FHx
- P/E:
  - Vitals - BP/P, RR, Temp
  - Abdominal - mass
  - PR - rectal masses?
  - Proctoscopy - look at any haemarrhoids
- Ix:
  - Blood tests:
    - CBC - anaemia, HcT
    - LRFT
    - Clotting and T&S
  - Specific Ix:
    - Imaging
    - Colonoscopy - diagnostic (localise the bleeder) and therapeutic (interventions)
- Approach to intermittent PR bleeding:
  - Hx - red flags:
    - GI Sx - altered bowel habits, tenesmus, mucus, duration of bleeding, blood mixed w/ stool, melaena or maroon coloured stool, older age
    - Constitutional Sx
    - Older age
    - FHx
  - P/E - DRE, proctoscopy
  - Ix:
    - CBC - anaemia (but unlikely caused by haemarrhoid alone)
    - CEA - low sensitivity
    - Imaging:
      - Double contrast barium enema (obselete)
      - Computed tomographic colonography (virtual colonoscopy):
        - Accepted alternative to colonoscopy - high sensitivity (\>90%)
    - Colonoscopy - gold standard:
      - Requires diet and mechanical bowel preparation +/- cessation of antithrombotics and sedation
    - Sigmoidoscopy - simpler preparation, and observation studies does not miss proximal cancer for outlet type bleeding
    - FOBT - guaiac based vs immunochemical, used as a screening tool
- Approach to acute lower GI bleeding:
  - Clinical presentation - round the clock massive bleeding +/- signs of shock
  - Etiology:
    - LGIT - 70% stop spontaneously
    - UGIB (15%)
  - Initial Mx:
    - Resuscitation - large bore IV cannula, fluid resuscitation +/- restrictive transfusion
    - O2 supplement
    - Foley - monitor hourly UO
    - Initial Ix - Haemoccue, CBC, LRFT, clotting, T&S
    - Stop anticoagulant and antiplatelet
    - Localisation
    - Theraupeutic
  - Endoscopy:
    - Procedure - no need bowel preparation (blood is cathartic), minimal preparation, but quite difficult due to poor visualisation
    - Therapeutic options:
      - Diverticular - TTS endoscopic clips
      - Angiodysplasia - argon plasma coagulation
      - Post polypectomy bleeding - mechanical clips or heater probes
  - Imaging - evaluation of SB after bleeding, requires active bleeding:
    - RBC scan
    - CT angiogram
    - Angiogram - detect extravastation of contrast by cannulation of SMA, IMA, and coeliac
- Approach to GI bleeding of obscure origin:
  - Definition - bleeding source not readily identified by conventional means (OGD, colonoscopy):
    - Occult - refractory Fe deficiency anaemia, +ve FOBT
    - Overt - observable bleeding
  - Etiology:
    - Vascular - Angiodysplasia. watermelon stomach, dieulafoy lesion
    - SB pathologies
  - Ix:
    - SB workup - VTE, SB enteroscopy, contrast CT angiogram, barium follow-trough

## Etiology of PR bleeding

- Anal - haemorrhoids, anal fissure, cancer
- Colorectal - cancer, rectal ulcers, diverticular bleeding, angiodysplasia, IBD, radiation proctitis, ischaemic bowel, infective colitis, post polypectomy bleeding
- SB - Meckel's diverticulum, Angiodysplasia, haemangioma, SB tumour, SB ulcer, Crohn's, Aortoenteric fistula
- Any caue of profuse UGIB

## Diverticular bleeding

- Epidemiology - only occurs w/ 17% patients w/ diverticulosis
- Pathophysiology - ruptured vasa rectum, bleeding from a single diverticulum which resolves spontaneously (80-85%), with rebleeding risk (20-30%)
- Clinical presentation - acute and severe LGIB
- Mx - resuscitation, endoscopic vs surgical resection

## Angiodysplasia

- Epidemiology - disease of the elderly
- Pathophysiology - caused by ectasia of submucosal vessels usually on right colon, ocassionally a/w vascular malformations
- Resolution - spontaneous in 85-90%, rebleeding risk varies (25-85%)
- ?Clinical Presentation

## IBD

- Clinical presentation - typically mild in nature (bloody mucous diarrhoea), 6-10% of UC present with acute severe LGIB

## Radiation proctitis

- Pathophysiology - radiation to rectal mucosa (prostate or cervical CA) and formation of telangiectasia
- Mx:
  - Resuscitation
  - Medical Tx - steroid, sucralfate enema
  - Argon plasma coagulation, RFA, formalin, laser, stoma diversion

## Rectal ulcer

- Epidemiology - more common in Asia, especially in elderly, critically ill and bedridden patient
- Pathophysiology - ?form of stress ulcer?
- Clinical presentation - sudden severe,painless +/- shock
- Mx - bedside:
  - Packing w/ adrenaline-soaked gauze
  - Suture plication
  - Endoscopic electrocoagulation
